WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.417
This video covers power management, the networking and wireless basics a meeting device needs, and the security habits both postings ask for.

00:00:09.617 --> 00:00:15.167
A bar runs warm and a call gets choppy when someone starts a big file download on the same network.

00:00:15.417 --> 00:00:19.213
Power, networking and security all touch that one moment.

00:00:19.463 --> 00:00:20.856
Let's tour each quickly.

00:00:21.906 --> 00:00:24.252
Running a device is like running a building.

00:00:24.652 --> 00:00:28.460
Turn off the lights in empty rooms. That's runtime power management.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:34.769
Runtime PM / suspend: Turning parts of the device off when idle and back on when needed. Like: Motion-sensor lights for each room of the house.

00:00:35.019 --> 00:00:39.385
But never plunge a busy room into darkness. That's what usage counts protect.

00:00:39.785 --> 00:00:46.309
Lock every door, including the side door the builders used. Those are JTAG, the UART console, and adb.

00:00:46.709 --> 00:00:55.603
JTAG / SWD: Hardware debug ports that let a debugger stop the processor and read its memory. Like: A doctor's stethoscope plugged straight into the chip.

00:00:55.903 --> 00:01:03.716
UART: A simple serial port, often the first console you see when a board boots. Like: A tin-can telephone straight into the board.

00:01:04.016 --> 00:01:13.501
adb: Android Debug Bridge: the command-line tool that gives an engineer a shell and logs on a device. Like: A service hatch into the device for the technician.

00:01:13.801 --> 00:01:20.628
And check every visitor's badge. That means validating every input from USB, HDMI and the network.

00:01:21.028 --> 00:01:29.480
HDMI: The common cable standard for sending video and audio to TVs and monitors. Like: A garden hose for pictures and sound.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:31.277
Words.

00:01:31.677 --> 00:01:36.042
Runtime power management turns individual devices off when idle.

00:01:36.442 --> 00:01:41.446
Autosuspend waits a short delay first, so devices don't flap on and off.

00:01:41.846 --> 00:01:48.719
USB link power management uses U one and U two states. Their exit latency can glitch isochronous streams.

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:54.030
LPM / U1 / U2: Link power management: USB links nap between packets to save power. Like: Lights on a motion sensor that switch off when the hallway is empty.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:02:07.252
Isochronous transfer: A USB transfer type with guaranteed timing but no retries, used for live video and audio. Like: A live TV broadcast: always on time, and a dropped frame is simply skipped, never resent.

00:02:07.552 --> 00:02:10.884
The trusted execution environment protects keys.

00:02:11.284 --> 00:02:16.938
And c f g eight oh two eleven and Blue Z are Linux's Wi-Fi framework and Bluetooth stack.

00:02:17.338 --> 00:02:23.271
Wi-Fi / Bluetooth: Short-range radio links: Wi-Fi for network data, Bluetooth for headsets and accessories. Like: A home phone line and a walkie-talkie.

00:02:23.521 --> 00:02:31.276
Framework layer: On Android, the Java system services apps talk to, like the camera and USB services. Like: The dining room of a restaurant: diners (apps) only ever see this room.

00:02:32.326 --> 00:02:36.100
Runtime power management for a camera sensor, step by step.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.729
An app opens the video device.

00:02:39.129 --> 00:02:43.401
The driver calls p m runtime get sync. The usage count becomes one.

00:02:43.801 --> 00:02:48.422
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:02:48.672 --> 00:02:55.603
The resume callback turns on regulators and clocks, and releases reset, in the order the datasheet requires.

00:02:56.003 --> 00:02:58.592
While streaming, the usage count keeps it on.

00:02:58.992 --> 00:03:00.792
The app closes the device.

00:03:01.192 --> 00:03:04.036
The driver puts its reference, with autosuspend.

00:03:04.436 --> 00:03:14.734
After the delay, the suspend callback turns clocks and regulators off. Unbalanced gets and puts are the classic bug: devices that never sleep, or that turn off mid stream.

00:03:15.934 --> 00:03:18.326
Networking basics for meeting devices.

00:03:18.726 --> 00:03:25.669
Know ip, ethtool and tcpdump, and mark real time media with D S C P so switches prioritize it.

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:30.608
Enterprise networks use VLANs and eight oh two dot one X authentication.

00:03:31.008 --> 00:03:36.813
For Wi-Fi: i w, w p a supplicant, and where chip firmware is loaded from.

00:03:37.213 --> 00:03:43.889
Firmware: Software that runs close to hardware, usually stored in flash memory on the device. Like: The instincts built into an animal: low-level behaviour that is there from birth.

00:03:44.139 --> 00:03:51.639
For Bluetooth: capture the host controller interface with b t mon, and know the headset profiles, including L E audio.

00:03:52.839 --> 00:03:54.139
Security habits.

00:03:54.539 --> 00:03:59.264
Lock JTAG and the UART in production, and disable adb in user builds.

00:03:59.664 --> 00:04:05.841
Treat every USB descriptor, E D I D block and network packet as hostile, and fuzz the parsers.

00:04:06.241 --> 00:04:09.480
Descriptor: A small table of facts the device reports about itself: who made it, what it can do. Like: A business card plus resume the device hands over when it is first plugged in.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:22.687
EDID / HPD: EDID is the screen's description of what it can show; hot-plug detect (HPD) is the wire that says a screen is connected. Like: A guest's dietary card (EDID) and the doorbell that says they arrived (HPD).

00:04:22.987 --> 00:04:26.180
Keep keys in the trusted environment, never in files.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.955
Use signed updates, verified boot, d m verity, and enforcing SELinux.

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:42.781
AVB / dm-verity: Android Verified Boot and dm-verity: checks that the system partitions have not been changed, block by block. Like: A tamper seal on every page of the rulebook.

00:04:43.081 --> 00:04:56.548
SELinux: Security-Enhanced Linux: rules that say exactly which program may touch which file or device, even as root. Like: Keycards in an office: even the boss's card only opens the doors it is programmed for.

00:04:56.848 --> 00:05:01.283
And keep a software bill of materials, with monthly security patch merges.

00:05:02.483 --> 00:05:09.960
Question for you: Live audio stutters when someone downloads a big file on the same network. What network feature helps?

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:22.482
Answer: Quality of service marking, so audio and video packets get priority over bulk download traffic.

00:05:22.782 --> 00:05:30.166
Bulk transfer: A USB transfer type that is reliable but has no timing promise, used for files and updates. Like: Parcel post: everything arrives intact, but not at a promised minute.

00:05:31.216 --> 00:05:31.912
Recap.

00:05:32.312 --> 00:05:34.460
Balance every runtime get with a put.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:38.215
Watch USB link power management when streams glitch.

00:05:38.615 --> 00:05:40.902
Mark media traffic for priority.

00:05:41.302 --> 00:05:43.439
Close the debug doors in production.

00:05:43.839 --> 00:05:46.532
And validate and fuzz every external input.
